perimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Jerusalem","confidence":0.99}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Jerusalem became the Holy City and the perceived “center of the world” through three thousand years of faith, slaughter, fanaticism, and coexistence.","confidence":0.78},"academicOrientation":{"score":30,"confidence":0.86},"profileConfidence":0.9,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Middle East","confidence":0.55}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"bfcaed18acae273bcf244918130f47e270e3441253d8e3260b4efe28efd77897","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"Jerusalem is the universal city, the capital of two peoples, the shrine of three faiths; it is the prize of empires, the site of Judgement Day and the battlefield of today's clash of civilizations. From King David to Barack Obama, from the birth of Judaism, Christianity and Islam to the Israel-Palestine conflict, this is the epic history of three thousand years of faith, slaughter, fanaticism and coexistence. How did this small, remote town become the Holy City, the \"center of the world\" and now the key to peace in the Middle East? In a gripping narrative, Simon Sebag Montefiore reveals this ever-changing city in its many incarnations, bringing every epoch and character blazingly to life. Jerusalem's biography is told through the wars, love affairs and revelations of the men and women -- kings, empresses, prophets, poets, saints, conquerors and whores -- who created, destroyed, chronicled and believed in Jerusalem. As well as the many ordinary Jerusalemites who have left their mark on the city, its cast varies from Solomon, Saladin and Suleiman the Magnificent to Cleopatra, Caligula and Churchill; from Abraham to Jesus and Muhammad; from the ancient world of Jezebel, Nebuchadnezzar, Herod and Nero to the modern times of the Kaiser, Disraeli, Mark Twain, Lincoln, Rasputin, Lawrence of Arabia and Moshe Dayan.","su